\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s10 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Class Description \
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 An NSActionCell defines an active area inside a control (an instance of NSControl or one of its subclasses). As an NSControl\'27s active area, an NSActionCell does three things: it usually performs display of text or an icon; it provides the NSControl with a target and an action; and it handles mouse (cursor) tracking by properly highlighting its area and sending action messages to its target based on cursor movement. The only way to specify the NSControl for a particular NSActionCell is to send the NSActionCell a \b drawWithFrame:inView:\b0  message, passing the NSControl as the argument for the \b inView:\b0  keyword of the method.\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSActionCell implements the target object and action method as defined by its superclass, NSCell. As a user manipulates an NSControl, NSActionCell\'27s \b trackMouse:inRect:ofView:untilMouseUp:\b0  method (inherited from NSCell) updates its appearance and sends the action message to the target object with the NSControl object as the only argument. See "Target and Action" below more more on this paradigm.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Usually, the responsibility for an NSControl\'27s appearance and behavior is completely given over to a corresponding NSActionCell. (NSMatrix, and its subclass NSForm, are NSControls that don\'27t follow this rule.)\
\fs14\
\fs28 A single NSControl may have more than one NSActionCell. To help identify it in this case, every NSActionCell has an integer tag. Note, however, that no checking is done by the NSActionCell object itself to ensure that the tag is unique. See the NSMatrix class for an example of a subclass of NSControl that contains multiple NSActionCells.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Many of the methods that define the contents and look of an NSActionCell, such as \b setFont:\b0  and \b setBordered:\b0 , are reimplementations of methods inherited from NSCell. They\'27re overriden to ensure that the NSActionCell is redisplayed when "visual" attributes change.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s57 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Target and Action\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Target objects and action methods (or messages) are part of the mechanism by which NSControls respond to user actions and enable users to communicate their intentions to an application. A target is an object that an NSControl uses as the receiver of action messages. The target\'27s class defines an action method to enable its instances to respond to these messages, which are sent as users click or otherwise manipulate the NSControl. NSControl\'27s \b sendAction:to:\b0  asks the NSApplication object, NSApp, to send an action message to the NSControl\'27s target object.\
\fs14\
\fs28 An action method takes only one argument: the \b id\b0  of the sender. The sender may be either the NSControl that sends the action message or, on occassion, another object that the target should treat as the sender. When it receives an action message, a target can return messages to the sender requesting additional information about its status.  \
\fs14\
\fs28 You can also set the target to \b nil\b0  and allow it to be determined at run time. When the target is \b nil\b0 , the NSApplication object must look for an appropriate receiver. It conducts its search in a prescribed order, by following the responder chain until it finds an object that can respond to the message:\
\fs14\
\pard \s5 \li1810 \fi-298 \ri1007 \ql \tx1810 \tx6300 \tx6590 \tx15154 \fs28 \'b7\tab It begins with the first responder in the key window and follows \b nextResponder\b0  links up the responder chain to the NSWindow\'27s content view. \
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab It tries the NSWindow object and then the NSWindow\'27s delegate.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab If the main window is different from the key window, it then starts over with the first responder in the main window and works its way up the main window\'27s responder chain to the NSWindow object and its delegate.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab Next, the NSApplication object tries to respond itself. If it can\'27t respond, it tries its own delegate. NSApp and its delegate are the receivers of last resort.\
\fs14\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \fs28 NSControl provides methods for setting and using the target object and the action method. However, these methods require that an NSControl\'27s cell (or cells) be NSActionCells or custom cells that hold action and target as instance variables and can respond to the NSControl methods. \
\fs14\
